    text: `I stuffed a shirt or two into my old carpet-bag, tucked it under my arm, and started for Cape Horn and the Pacific. Quitting the good city of old Manhattan, I duly arrived in New Bedford. It was on a Saturday night in December. Much was I disappointed. The last sailing packet for Nantucket had already sailed, and no way of reaching that place offered, save by a voyage of some days duration. I was a stranger in the place, and my pocket was in a sad state of consumption. New Bedford is a queer place of hills and valleys, and the streets are very wide, and in winter they are very cold. I began to look about for a lodging.

I went from one sign to another, but they all looked too expensive and jolly. At last I came to a very dark and dreary corner, where a small house stood, with a dim light burning in the window. It looked like a place that would suit my purse. It was the Spouter-Inn: Peter Coffin. The landlord was a quiet man who did not say much, but he had a very warm fire burning in the hearth, and that was just what I needed.

I entered the bar-room, which was filled with sailors from all parts of the world. They were drinking and talking, and the noise was deafening. I sat down in a corner and tried to dry my clothes, which were wet from the snow that was falling outside. I felt very lonely and miserable, and I began to wonder if I had made a mistake in leaving my comfortable home to go to sea. But then I remembered that I was an adventurer, and that adventures are always full of difficulties. I resolved to make the best of things, and to face whatever might come with a brave heart.

The Spouter-Inn was indeed a curious place. The walls were decorated with all kinds of whaling gear, including harpoons, lances, and whalebones. There were also several paintings of whales and ships, which looked very old and dusty. The bar itself was made of a huge piece of whale's jaw, and the bartender was a rough-looking man with a wooden leg.

I ordered a cup of hot tea and a piece of bread, and as I ate, I watched the other people in the room. Most of them were whalemen who had just returned from long voyages in the Pacific or the Arctic. They were telling stories of their adventures, of the whales they had chased and the storms they had survived. Their voices were loud and deep, and they laughed heartily at their own jokes.

As I sat there, I felt a sudden sense of excitement. These were the men I was going to sail with, and these were the adventures I was going to share. I forgot my loneliness and my coldness, and my heart beat faster with anticipation. I was ready to begin my new life as a whaleman.

Suddenly, the door opened and a tall man entered the room. He was dressed in a dark coat and carried a heavy sea-chest on his shoulder. He walked over to the landlord and asked for a room.

"I have only one bed left," said the landlord, "and you will have to share it with a harpooneer."

The newcomer hesitated for a moment, but then he nodded. "I don't mind," he said. "As long as he is a quiet fellow."

"He is quiet enough," said the landlord, "and he is one of the best harpooneers in the business. His name is Queequeg."

I watched the tall man as he went up the stairs, carrying his sea-chest. I wondered what kind of a man Queequeg was, and if I would get along with him. Little did I know then that Queequeg would become my best friend and my companion in many dangerous adventures. I decided to retire for the night, and as I lay in my bed, listening to the wind howling outside, I felt a deep sense of peace. I was on my way to Cape Horn, and the vast ocean was waiting for me.`
