    text: `It was the Dover road that lay, on a Friday night late in November, before the first of the persons with whom this history has business. The Dover road lay, as to him, beyond the Dover mail, as it lumbered up Shooter's Hill. He walked up hill in the mire by the side of the mail, as the rest of the passengers did; not because they had any inclination for a walk, but because the hill, and the harness, and the mud, and the mail, were all so heavy that the horses had three times already come to a stop, with an obstinate determination of going no further.

With drooping heads and tremulous tails, they stood under the commanding influence of the coachman, who was quite certain that one or more of his horses would fall down if he let them stop for a minute. The passengers, indeed, had been warned that if they did not get out and walk, the coach would be overturned, and as nobody wanted to spend the night in a muddy ditch, they had all got out and were now walking up the hill, shivering in the cold damp air.

It was very dark, and a thick mist covered the road, so that it was impossible to see more than a few feet ahead. The coachman could only see the ears of his lead horses, and even those were dimmed by the mist. He cracked his whip and shouted to the horses, but they only moved slowly and with great difficulty. The passengers walked in silence, each wrapped in his own thoughts, and keeping a close watch on the dark woods that lined the road, for the Dover road was known to be infested with highwaymen, and everyone was afraid of being attacked.

Jerry Cruncher, the messenger from Tellson's Bank, was riding on horseback behind the mail. He had been sent to deliver a message to Mr. Lorry, one of the passengers in the coach. Jerry was a rough-looking man with a hoarse voice and a dark, secretive manner. He kept his horse at a distance from the coach, not wanting to attract the attention of the guard or the other passengers, who were suspicious of anyone riding behind them in the dark.

As the coach neared the top of the hill, the mist began to clear a little, and Mr. Lorry looked out of the window. He was an elderly gentleman, dressed in a neat brown suit, with a wig that was slightly askew. He had been sleeping fitfully during the journey, and his face was tired and pale. He looked at the dark woods and the misty road, and felt a sudden chill of fear. He knew that the journey was dangerous, and he wished that he were safe at home in London.

Suddenly, the guard called out: "Who goes there? Stand and declare yourself!"

Jerry Cruncher rode up to the side of the coach, his horse panting and covered with foam. He raised his hand to show that he was unarmed.

"I have a message for Mr. Jarvis Lorry," he said, "from Tellson's Bank in London."

Mr. Lorry opened the window and looked out. "I am Jarvis Lorry," he said. "What is the message?"

Jerry handed him a small folded paper. Mr. Lorry opened it and read the words: "Wait at Dover for Mam'selle."

He looked at Jerry in surprise. "Is that all?" he asked.

"Yes, sir," said Jerry. "That is the message I was told to deliver."

Mr. Lorry nodded and closed the window. He turned to the other passengers, who were watching him with anxious eyes. "It is a business message," he said, "from my bank. There is nothing to fear."

The coachman cracked his whip, and the horses started off again, moving faster now that they were on the flat road at the top of the hill. Jerry Cruncher watched the coach lumber away into the darkness, and then turned his horse's head back toward London. He felt a sense of relief that his task was done, but he also felt a curious unease. He wondered what the message meant, and who the "Mam'selle" was that Mr. Lorry was to wait for at Dover. Little did he know then how deeply his own life would become involved in the strange and terrible events that were about to unfold.`
